      "assetLineKey": "Reaper:10:N01"
    },
    {
      "blockKey": "replacement",
      "text": "What remained deserved a future, even if it could never become its former self.",
      "mode": "NARRATED",
      "textSHA256": "334f3db0e5f726e95068fe895cb696b2045de0454f63e6efb9dc51c997c02e93",
      "assetLineKey": "Reaper:10:N02"
    },
    {
      "blockKey": "evidence",
      "text": "The old portrait could help them recognize him. It could not answer on behalf of the existence that had endured. The Reaper had already met echoes whose names were gone and survivors whose pasts could not agree. Insisting upon a cleaner account had repeatedly concealed the person still present.",
      "mode": "SILENT_HANDWRITING",
      "textSHA256": "00c7bd4ae09368345faeb9121414c0f52abdcfe4f45f0870ac00250c64de2760"
    },
    {
      "blockKey": "account",
      "text": "This is no acceptance of the prison, and no judgment that it must last. Freedom remains worth seeking. Its purpose must include the possibility of an answer they did not expect. If returning their friend required silencing whatever he had become, preservation would once again be mistaken for care.",
      "mode": "SILENT_HANDWRITING",
      "textSHA256": "c92fe741c64d534f597df106ff79834d8a3f75e318a4cb3fe4b1af0a03fcb630"
    },
    {
      "blockKey": "closing",
      "text": "They could search for a way to free him. They could not decide in advance who he must be when he answered.",
      "mode": "NARRATED",
      "textSHA256": "e60866c9c6662a4da5ce3c0a8fd6d25ee2e5e9560f39c067299e1c9946563812",
      "assetLineKey": "Reaper:10:N03"
    }
  ],
  "journalText": "The Reaper had been arranging the surviving details into a portrait of the friend before the breaking. Every match seemed useful; every difference seemed another injury to remove. Then the habit of making room returned within the merged recollection, alongside a resistance he could not attribute with the same confidence.\n\nTo save what remained, they had to recover the fourth companion exactly as he had been.\n\nWhat remained deserved a future, even if it could never become its former self.\n\nThe old portrait could help them recognize him. It could not answer on behalf of the existence that had endured. The Reaper had already met echoes whose names were gone and survivors whose pasts could not agree. Insisting upon a cleaner account had repeatedly concealed the person still present.\n\nThis is no acceptance of the prison, and no judgment that it must last. Freedom remains worth seeking. Its purpose must include the possibility of an answer they did not expect. If returning their friend required silencing whatever he had become, preservation would once again be mistaken for care.\n\nThey could search for a way to free him. They could not decide in advance who he must be when he answered.",
  "wordCount": 200,
  "newlyRecoveredInformation": "The rescue's ethical aim shifts from compulsory restoration of a remembered portrait to freedom and an open future for the surviving existence. Fusion was already known and is not presented as the new discovery.",
  "newlyAuthoredDetails": [
    "The Reaper's recovered comparison between a pre-Severing portrait and a surviving habit. Inability to reverse fusion is not asserted as fact; the if clause preserves uncertainty."
  ],
